# Which keys may sign, and under which names. Two mechanisms decide, and they
# are NOT the same one -- getting them confused is the trap this file sets:
#
#   LOCALLY, `git verify-commit` matches by KEY. ssh-keygen returns the FIRST
#   line whose key matches the signature; the committer's email plays no part
#   at all. Measured: a commit authored with the GitLab address verifies
#   against the GitHub principal, and swapping the two lines swaps the name git
#   prints. What a line really contributes is its valid-after window, which IS
#   applied, against the signature's date.
#
#   ON THE FORGE, the email is everything: GitHub and GitLab attribute a commit
#   and grant it "Verified" by the address it carries, not by this file.
#
# So the second principal is inert today, and kept deliberately: it records
# that both identities are mine, and it is the line that would carry a
# per-forge signing key the day one exists. Same valid-after on both -- one
# key, created once.
#
# Maintained by `./run install gitsign`, which appends what is missing and
# never rewrites a line: a valid-before added by hand during a rotation stays.
